{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-sourcetype-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the SOURCE TYPE dimension at width five: the `expand-sourcetype` run computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. Source types 11 (motorcycle), 21 (passenger car), 31 (passenger truck), 51 (refuse truck) and 62 (combination long-haul truck) -- two light-duty, one heavy-duty vocational, one long-haul and a motorcycle, which is as far apart as this database's physics gets.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES over `expand-fueltype-diesel`, which is the same axis at width two.\n\n* FIVE DRIVE CYCLES RATHER THAN TWO, and five that disagree. `sourceusetypephysics` gives each source type its own rolling, rotating and drag terms and its own fixed mass factor, so the vehicle-specific power of a drive-schedule second, the operating mode it bins into and the speed-bin brackets differ across all five. A motorcycle and a combination long-haul truck do not share an operating-mode distribution.\n\n* A COHORT COUNT THAT IS A SUM OVER FIVE UNEQUAL TERMS. 430 rows: 41 + 125 + 125 + 89 + 50. Source type 11 carries one fuel and 41 model years; 21 and 31 carry four fuels each and reach the full 125; 51 and 62 carry fuels the database has fewer sample vehicles for. 656 candidates in the grid, 430 survivors, and no declaration anywhere says 430 -- `cohortSurvivorCount` recomputes it and `tools/check-runspec-shape.py` holds the emitted relation to it.\n\nTHE TEN SELECTIONS, ONE BINDING AND THE ORACLE are everything this document carries, and it differs from `runspecs/expand-fueltype-diesel.esm` in eleven `url_template`s alone.\n\nWHY THIS DOCUMENT IS IN `runspecs/pending/` AND NOT `runspecs/`, as of 2026-09-18. It is complete and it validates; the model does not yet reproduce it, for ONE reason that is not the source-type axis. A MOVES source bin is (sourceTypeID, fuelTypeID, modelYearID, regClassID, engTechID) and this model's cohort candidate stops at the first three. Source type 21 has a single regulatory class (20) for every (model year, fuel) it carries, so the distinction was invisible for every run before this one; source type 32 has TWO for 333 of its 444 triples (30 and 41), 51 has up to four and 62 up to three. `coh_regClassID` is a `sum_product` over the matching `samplevehiclepopulation` rows, so on such a triple it returns 71 rather than 30 or 41, `emissionrate` matches nothing and the rate is 0.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY PROVEN BY RUNNING IT, which is why it is checked in rather than deleted: the emitted relation is the right SHAPE -- 248 rows, 125 under source type 21 and 123 under 32, from a declaration the chain recomputes -- and every one of source type 21's 125 rows reproduces `MOVESOutput` at worst cell 7.106e-06, the same worst cell `expand-day` reports. The axis works; the cohort is too coarse. Source type 32's 23 single-regulatory-class rows are wrong too, at 0.735, and they have the same cause one step back: their E85 activity is re-attributed from a gasoline cohort whose `coh_regClassID` is the 71, so `coh_usedFraction` finds no partner.\n\nWHAT IT WAITS ON: the cohort candidate grid gaining the regulatory class as a third factor, `coh_rank` ranking distinct (source type, fuel type, model year) TRIPLES rather than candidates, and the output relation's ordinal join summing the bins of a triple -- which the `faq` already does, so the output side is the small half. PLAN.md §7.3.5 has the measurement.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-day"
    ]
  },
